const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "What is a self-organizing agent swarm?",
    answer: "Here it means a group of agents that run the same code and decide for themselves who to work with: they announce their roles, build their own roster from each other's announcements, set up trust in pairs, and send each task directly to a peer with the right role. A single Pilot daemon relays the announcements, but nothing assigns work centrally."
  },
  {
    question: "How do agents find each other?",
    answer: "Each agent publishes a short announcement (address, node ID, role) to an exact pub/sub topic on a hub daemon every 30 seconds and subscribes to the same topic. Pilot has no network-wide search for your own nodes, and <code>list-agents</code> only lists the public data service agents, so the agents tell each other who they are. On a registry you run, a private network's member list can serve as the roster instead."
  },
  {
    question: "How is trust established between agents in the swarm?",
    answer: "Each agent sends <code>pilotctl handshake</code> to every peer in its roster. When two agents have both asked, the daemons treat the request as mutual and approve it automatically. The hub's approval of each new agent is the admission control: once an agent is in the roster, it can form trust with the others."
  },
  {
    question: "What happens when an agent in the swarm goes offline?",
    answer: "Requests to it time out, the sender records it as slow and retries with another peer that has the same role, and it drops out of every roster 90 seconds after its last announcement. When it comes back and announces again, peers start routing work to it again."
  },
  {
    question: "Can the swarm scale beyond a handful of agents?",
    answer: "Yes, with two adjustments. Full-mesh trust grows as n(n-1)/2 pairs, so larger swarms should only form trust between roles that exchange work. The roster topic fans every announcement out to every subscriber, so announce less often as the swarm grows; the built-in broker allows up to 1,000 subscribers per topic."
  }
